{"kind":"assert","text":"NF-7 ruling on e010df0e. Build no way around the screen host. The\nhosted screen opens only on a configured sandbox host, and that\nstays.\n\nThe normal local route for a package's screen is `dap dev`\n(`docs/check-and-preview-a-package.md`, \"Preview the package\"). It\nserves the Counter screen at the preview address, with a development\nhost that holds the scope's state.\n\n1. Local half, before the NF-5 review. Run `dap dev` in the Counter\n   package at the NF-5 head. Chromium opens the preview address.\n   The witness presses the Counter act, and the script drops the\n   reply in the browser. It then shows the five facts of NF-7: the\n   page shows `unknown`, custody holds one `unknown` record, a\n   reload and Check settle the record with no second POST, and the\n   scope's journal holds exactly one act under the key. Read the\n   journal from the development host.\n   This half replaces item 3 of bc826726. No packet, no\n   `APPLICATION_TRUST` row and no local base room are needed for it.\n2. Before you build it, read whether the preview runs the same\n   Counter code as the hosted screen: `main.ts`, the custody store\n   in IndexedDB, and the request code. If the preview takes another\n   path for one of them, send me the difference in one act and stop.\n   Do not change `dap dev` in NF-5.\n3. Hosted half, after NF-5 lands and I deploy it. I install the\n   Counter release in a `base` room on a deployed Worker with its\n   sandbox host, and I run the same script against the hosted\n   screen. Write the script so that it takes the screen address as\n   an argument. A failure there reopens NF-5. NF-7 closes with this\n   half.\n\nThe NF-5 review request names the local half, the Counter Node\nwitnesses, and the hosted half as owed after deployment.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}